(Bug #65's control. Refusing an initialiser whose type is provably wrong must
 not narrow what a correct declaration is allowed to say. Every line below is
 a documented shape, and every one of them compiled and ran exactly like this
 before the fix too: this test exists to prove the fix took nothing away.)

(Each concrete type initialised with its own kind - LANGUAGE.md:450-458.)
a number called count is 42.
a float called ratio is 0.5.
a text called greeting is "hello".
a boolean called ready is true.
a list called items is [1, 2, 3].
a map called ages is {"bo": 42}.
print count.
print ratio.
print greeting.
print ready.
print items.
print ages.

(Every conversion the Basic Conversions table documents, LANGUAGE.md:1902-1918.
 All of them are explicit casts, which is the whole point: the cast is how a
 declaration says it meant to change the type.)
a number called truncated is 3.9 as a number.
a float called widened is 42 as a float.
a text called written is 25 as text.
a number called parsed is "123" as a number.
a float called measured is "3.5" as a float.
a number called flagged is true as a number.
a boolean called switched is 42 as a boolean.
a text called spelled is true as text.
a boolean called answered is "true" as a boolean.
print truncated.
print widened.
print written.
print parsed.
print measured.
print flagged.
print switched.
print spelled.
print answered.

(A value takes any type - the sanctioned dynamic mechanism, LANGUAGE.md:585-595.)
a value called slot is 5.
print slot.
set slot to "now it holds text".
print slot.

(A buffer takes a content write, not a typed value - LANGUAGE.md:581-584.)
a buffer called data is "seed".
print data.
a buffer called tally is 42.
print tally.

(A text declared from a buffer without the cast still compiles. What it
 prints is bug #51's open question - copy the bytes, or reject and name
 `as text` - which this fix deliberately did not decide, so this line asserts
 only that it is still accepted.)
a text called line is data.

(A file is opened from a text path and a time comes from the clock. Both give
 a handle a value of another kind, and LANGUAGE.md:503-519 makes both of them
 the canonical form - so neither is a mismatch to refuse.)
a file called source is "./input.txt".
a time called now is current time.
print "a file and a time still declare".

(Arithmetic that yields the declared type - and the mixing rule at
 LANGUAGE.md:1803, which is about expressions, so a float expression goes in
 a float.)
a number called total is 3 add 4.
a float called scaled is 1.5 multiply 2.
print total.
print scaled.

(A call's declared return type, matching the declaration it lands in.)
To 'name it'. Return text, "bo".
To 'count it'. Return a number, 7.

a text called nickname is 'name it'.
a number called counted is 'count it'.
print nickname.
print counted.

(A format string is text; a character literal is a number.)
a text called shown is "count = {count}".
a number called letter is 'A'.
print shown.
print letter.

(An element read whose type matches the declaration - bug #54's control,
 unchanged by this fix.)
a list called names is ["bo", "cy"].
a text called who is element 1 of names.
print who.

(An argument and a return that agree with what the function declares.)
To greet with a text called who. Print who.

greet with "hello from an argument".
